WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.581 --> 00:00:02.671
- [Narrator] Ah, Mother's
Day, the one day a year

00:00:02.671 --> 00:00:05.052
where people all of the
world pay tribute to mom,

00:00:05.052 --> 00:00:06.469
mama, mum, or ma.

00:00:07.902 --> 00:00:10.212
But how did it all begin?

00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:12.417
Enter Anna Jarvis, the
founder of Mother's Day

00:00:12.417 --> 00:00:15.417
who came to despise her own holiday.

00:00:18.071 --> 00:00:21.029
Anna Jarvis really,
really loved her mother.

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:22.938
- [Katharine] Her mother dies in 1905,

00:00:22.938 --> 00:00:26.597
and in 1907 she really
starts this campaign

00:00:26.597 --> 00:00:30.638
that we need to have a day
in honor of what mothers do.

00:00:30.638 --> 00:00:32.284
- [Narrator] That's
Dr. Katharine Antolini,

00:00:32.284 --> 00:00:34.415
she's an historian and
wrote a book about Anna.

00:00:34.415 --> 00:00:35.698
- [Katharine] She always
identified herself

00:00:35.698 --> 00:00:38.006
as Anna Jarvis, the
founder of Mother's Day.

00:00:38.006 --> 00:00:39.877
- [Narrator] And in 1914, after six years

00:00:39.877 --> 00:00:41.298
and state-by-state campaigning,

00:00:41.298 --> 00:00:43.167
Jarvis' dream came true:

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:44.747
President Woodrow Wilson
officially designated

00:00:44.747 --> 00:00:47.019
the second Sunday of May as Mother's Day.

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:48.186
Hooray, right?

00:00:51.415 --> 00:00:54.633
Jarvis felt Wilson was taking
credit for her efforts,

00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:56.927
and he wasn't the only source of her ire.

00:00:56.927 --> 00:00:59.024
- [Katharine] Anna had a
really wonderful enemies list.

00:00:59.024 --> 00:01:01.285
She was mad at the candy industry,

00:01:01.285 --> 00:01:03.157
and she was mad at the floral industry,

00:01:03.157 --> 00:01:05.168
and she was mad at all of these charities.

00:01:05.168 --> 00:01:06.657
- [Narrator] She also turned
on some of her early allies,

00:01:06.657 --> 00:01:09.247
including John Wanamaker,
a business tycoon

00:01:09.247 --> 00:01:11.365
who saw a commercial
opportunity in Mother's Day.

00:01:11.365 --> 00:01:12.957
He started putting the
holiday on everything,

00:01:12.957 --> 00:01:14.668
even dishes at his restaurant.

00:01:14.668 --> 00:01:16.585
Jarvis did not approve.

00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:20.984
- [Katharine] She ordered
this Mother's Day salad,

00:01:20.984 --> 00:01:22.229
and once the salad arrived,

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:24.644
Mrs. Jarvis just dumped it
on the floor and walked out.

00:01:24.644 --> 00:01:26.378
That was not was the
day was supposed to be.

00:01:26.378 --> 00:01:28.179
- [Narrator] What exactly
was the day supposed to be?

00:01:28.179 --> 00:01:29.711
- [Katharine] So, it
really wasn't meant to be,

00:01:29.711 --> 00:01:31.357
go out, rush out and
buy all these flowers,

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.298
and greeting cards, and candy, and gifts,

00:01:33.298 --> 00:01:35.174
it was just go home and spend
the day with your mother.

00:01:35.174 --> 00:01:36.588
That's all she wants.

00:01:36.588 --> 00:01:37.815
- [Narrator] I wonder
what Anna Jarvis would say

00:01:37.815 --> 00:01:39.388
about the way Mother's
Day is celebrated today.

00:01:39.388 --> 00:01:40.807
- [Katharine] She would
still be defending it

00:01:40.807 --> 00:01:42.594
against commercialization.

00:01:42.594 --> 00:01:44.694
We celebrate this holiday in ways that she

00:01:44.694 --> 00:01:46.355
doesn't think we should.

00:01:46.355 --> 00:01:47.228
- [Narrator] Hear that people?

00:01:47.228 --> 00:01:48.684
Don't forget about Anna Jarvis,

00:01:48.684 --> 00:01:49.874
the mother of Mother's Day,

00:01:49.874 --> 00:01:52.624
and please, send your mom a card.

00:01:54.275 --> 00:01:56.643
- [Katharine] For her, a
greeting card would be insincere,

00:01:56.643 --> 00:01:58.549
it would be cheap, it would be lazy.

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:00.967
If you can't go home, then, you know,
write a letter to her,

00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:02.017
not just send a card.

00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:03.837
- [Narrator] Got it, write
your mom a handwritten letter.

00:02:03.837 --> 00:02:06.587
She deserves it, don't you think?

